# Iridium coverage hexagon outline: the rim drawn round the patch of
# Earth one satellite can see, so the cells read as a grid of hexagons
# over the blue tint of demo/koppi/iridium-coverage.obj.  This file is
# shared by every satellite of the fleet and rotated just like the tint,
# local +z at the satellite, so the two always line up.
#
#   shape         the same hexagon as the tint: corners at 23.0 deg, a
#                 band from 97% of the way out to the boundary -- about
#                 0.60 deg of arc at the flats (66 km), a line on the globe
#   surface       built at radius 10.022, 14.02 km above the sphere of radius
#                 10 and 3.82 km above the tint; the tint's triangles dip
#                 to 10.0049, this rim's to 10.0196, so the rim draws over the
#                 tint everywhere (the generator asserts it)
#   geometry      144 triangles around the band, the boundary sampled every
#                 5.0 deg exactly as the tint's is
#
# Coordinates are bpp scene units: 1 unit = 637.1 km, north up; the mesh
# is centred on the scene origin, the Earth's centre, so a rotation is
# all it takes to place it -- no translation (see demo/koppi/iridium.lua).
#
# Faces are triangles, each corner carrying its face's own normal (out of
# the sphere); no texture coordinates, the colour comes from the object.
